A||||7||Loki||Bleeding brakes procedure?????||||1||
Z||000000||Loki||04-29-2001||02:51 PM||||OK, im in the process of replaceing front rotors and pads, to bleed brakes i have someone pump brake peddle till its nice and firm the break open bolt on caliper till? no bubbles? no more fluid? what?<P><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||04-29-2001||03:08 PM||||You have someone pump the pedal aprox 3 times and then hold it to the floor, while you crack the bleeder screw for a few seconds and then close it again. THEN they can let the pedal off the floor. If they let it up earlier, your doomed..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Make sure you keep the master cyl topped up. Keep doing that until there are no bubbles coming out (I attach a rubber line to the bleeder screw) and your brakes are firm.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-29-2001||03:11 PM||||*<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 04-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-29-2001||03:11 PM||||You need to loosen the bleeder screw, not the bolt which will leave air in the caliper when mounted on the car. Loosen the bleeder screw while someone depresses the brake pedal and holds it down, tighten the bleeder screw, and release the pedal. Repeat till there is no more air in the system, remember to keep an eye on the master cylinder-don't let it go dry!<BR>If you just replaced the pads and rotors and did not open the brakes hydralic system, you should have no air unless it was present before.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-29-2001||07:27 PM||||This is VERY Important! You need to start at the right rear first, then the left rear, then the right front, and finally the left front. If you don't you will have a hard time getting air out of the system.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||04-30-2001||08:46 AM||||The fiero has separate front and rear fluid systems. If he has only messed with the front he should not have to bleed the rear lines.<BR>(Unless someone else failed to bleed the rear lines properly)<P>  Correct?||||reg||1
Z||000006||lawrence||04-30-2001||12:58 PM||||I made my own "speed bleeder" screw that allows me to bleed the brakes by myself.  Here's a description of what I did:<P>  <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/98/4/we.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/98/4/we.htm</A> <P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-30-2001||07:59 PM||||If you are doing the pedal pump method....<P>DO NOT pump the pedal repeatedly or fast. You'll likely aerate the fluid which will then be a giant pain in the ass. You'll quickly go nuts with a full system bleed. Such as after air got into the MC. This is less of a problem with caliper only bleeds. <P>1. Slowly Press pedal and HOLD DOWN.<BR>2. Crack bleed open. let pedal drop to lowest point and hold it there.<BR>3. Close bleeder.<BR>4. SLOWLY let the pedal up.<BR>5. repeat untill system is clear.<P>||||reg||1
